== WHY PROOFS MATTER IN BOTLISH
Botlish turns several run-time failures into compile-time obligations. A
call that may fail with a declared error must be handled, admitted in the
caller's `errors` clause, or proven unable to fail; a call proven to always
fail is rejected; a context a function needs must be installed before it
is called; an affine value is used at most once. Where the compiler can
prove an obligation away, the program needs no handler; where it cannot,
the program must say what happens.

== THREE KINDS OF STATEMENT
    LANGUAGE GUARANTEE   what the semantics promise on every backend: for
                         example, std::list::at(xs, i) returns the element at i
                         or fails with IndexNotFound, never anything else.
    RECOGNIZED PATTERNS  what this compiler proves, and the program forms
                         it recognizes: for example, std::list::at(xs, i) inside
                         `loop i from 0 to xs.length():` cannot fail.
    LIMITATIONS          what may be true but is not proven today: for
                         example, an index computed into a local binding
                         and then guarded is not related to the length.

The recognized patterns and the limitations describe this implementation,
not the language: a later compiler may prove more. Every example on a proof
page is compiled by Compilerpedia's own check, so the pages cannot claim a
pattern the compiler does not prove.

== WHEN A PROOF FAILS
An unproven obligation is not a bug in the program. The remedies, best
first: restate the code in a recognized form (proof pages list them; see
principle.restate-in-proven-form), handle the error where it can occur, or
admit it in the function's `errors` clause and let the caller decide.

