== LANGUAGE GUARANTEE
std::list::at(xs, i) (written xs.at(i) after `import std::list`) returns the
element of xs at i when 0 <= i < std::list::length(xs), and otherwise
completes with the builtin declared error IndexNotFound -- an ordinary,
handleable error, never a crash, a default or a wrapped index. There is no
negative indexing: -1 is a missing index like any other.
std::mutable_array::at, std::mutable_array::set, std::mutable_vector::at and
std::str::char_at (character index) have the same contract against their own
length or capacity.

Because IndexNotFound is a declared error, every call must do one of three
things (rule.unhandled-errors): handle it (`on IndexNotFound:`), admit it in
the enclosing function's `errors` clause, or be proven unable to raise it.
A call proven to *always* raise it (a literal index past a literal list) is
rejected as KNOWN-ERROR, even with a handler (rule.known-error).

The code generators use the proof: natively a proven call has no
IndexNotFound path (a proven std::list::at is a bare element load, and a
function whose only fallible operations are proven ones stops being
fallible), and the Tcl compiler calls the native directly instead of
through the error-handling call path. The interpreter, the reference,
checks every access at run time.

== RECOGNIZED PATTERNS (PROVEN TODAY)
The index proof relates an index to the container's size only in these
forms:

  * a counted loop over the same container's own length:
        loop i from 0 to xs.length():        (and `from 0 through
            xs.at(i)                           xs.length() - 1`)
  * a guard on a name, against the same container's length, that dominates
    the access, in either form, with `return` or `fail` in the guard:
        if i < 0 or i >= xs.length():        if i >= 0 and i < xs.length():
            return ...                           xs.at(i)
        xs.at(i)
    The guarded name may be a parameter, a loop variable, or a local bound
    to a computed index (`t = i + 1`, `t = f(i)`): the guard and the read
    name the same value through it, or through an alias of it.
  * a length guard and a constant index:  if xs.length() > 0: xs.at(0)
  * a container of statically known size with an index of known range:
    a list literal (or an immutable binding of one) and a constant index,
    or any List whose type states its length, List[T]{length: N}
    (concept.list-length): a declared parameter, field or result, a guard
    `if xs.length() == N:`, the copy of a fixed layout array, or
    std::list::with_length. An index proven outside 0..N-1 is KNOWN-ERROR.
  * a container reached through a field, written as the same field path in
    the loop, guard or reduction and in the access (`s.items.length()` ...
    `s.items.at(i)`), or bound to a local name first (`xs = s.items`). A
    field path names one value however often it is written, so this proves
    a write through the field place too: `t.slots.set(i, v)`.
  * an index reduced into the container's own size, inline or through
    local names:
        xs.at(h.mod(xs.length()))          for any Int h, negative or huge
        a.at(bit_and(h, a.capacity() - 1)) after `if a.capacity() == 0:
                                           return ...`
    mod by the size returns only when the size is not 0, and then
    0 <= h.mod(n) < n. A mask m keeps bit_and(h, m) in 0..m when m >= 0, so
    the mask needs m >= 0 proven (a guard that the size is not 0, or m's own
    range) but no power of two.
  * the List a loop collected (with no break or continue) over a container
    has that container's length, so any of the forms above may use the
    length of another loop's List over the same List or MutableArray -- or
    the same MutableVector, when nothing can change the vector's length
    between the two loops: no push, pop, take or clear on it on the way
    from the one loop to the other (a swap only replaces an element), and,
    for a context's vector, no yield and no call that may reach the
    installed context -- of a function that requires it (directly or
    through a function it calls), or that runs or resumes a coroutine.
    Pushes before both loops or after both, in the later loop's own body,
    after the later loop in the branch or handler it is in, in the other
    branch, or on a copy of the vector (another value) are fine: a vector
    built with pushes, then walked twice, has one length.

@example accept
import std::list

fn total(xs: List[int]) -> int:
    sums = loop i from 0 to xs.length():
        xs.at(i)
    sums.length()

total([4, 5, 6])
@caption A counted loop over the list's own length
@value 3
@end

@example accept
import std::list

fn pick(xs: List[int], t: int) -> int:
    if t < 0 or t >= xs.length():
        return -1
    xs.at(t)

[10, 20, 30].pick(1)
@caption A guard on a parameter dominates the read
@value 20
@end

@example accept
import std::list

fn corners(xs: List[int]{length: 4}) -> int:
    xs.at(0) + xs.at(3)

[1, 2, 3, 4].corners()
@caption A declared exact length and constant indices
@value 5
@end

@example accept
import std::list

fn after(xs: List[int], i: int) -> int:
    t = i + 1
    if t < 0 or t >= xs.length():
        return -1
    xs.at(t)

[10, 20, 30].after(1)
@caption A guard on a local bound to a computed index
@value 30
@end

@example accept
import std::list

struct Board:
    cells: List[int]

fn doubled(b: Board) -> List[int]:
    cells = b.cells
    loop i from 0 to cells.length():
        cells.at(i) * 2

doubled(Board {cells: [1, 2, 3]})
@caption A field's list, bound to a local name first
@value [2, 4, 6]
@end

@example accept
import std::mutable_array

fn slot(a: MutableArray[int], h: int) -> int:
    a.at(h.mod(a.capacity()))

std::mutable_array::from_list([10, 20, 30]).slot(-4)
@caption An index reduced mod the array's own capacity
@value 30
@end

@example accept
import std::mutable_array

struct Table:
    slots: MutableArray[int]

fn store(t: Table, h: int) -> Table:
    t.slots.set(h.mod(t.slots.capacity()), h)
    t

t = Table {slots: std::mutable_array::create(4, 0)}.store(7)
loop i from 0 to t.slots.capacity():
    t.slots.at(i)
@caption A write and a loop through a field path, written each time
@value [0, 0, 0, 7]
@end

@example accept
import std::list
import std::mutable_vector

fn scaled(h: int) -> int:
    v = std::mutable_vector::from_list([1, 2, 3])
    ys = loop x in v:
        x * 10
    zs = loop x in v:
        x
    ys.at(h.mod(zs.length()))

scaled(4)
@caption Two loops over a vector nothing mutates have one length
@value 20
@end

@example accept
import std::list
import std::mutable_vector

fn built(h: int) -> int:
    v = std::mutable_vector::from_list([1])
    loop i from 2 to 5:
        v.push(i)
    ys = loop x in v:
        x * 10
    zs = loop x in v:
        x
    ys.at(h.mod(zs.length()))

built(6)
@caption Built with pushes, then walked twice: nothing between the loops
@value 30
@end

@example accept
import std::list
import std::mutable_vector

fn branched(h: int) -> int:
    v = std::mutable_vector::from_list([1, 2, 3])
    ys = loop x in v:
        x * 10
    if h > 0:
        zs = loop x in v:
            x
        r = ys.at(h.mod(zs.length()))
        v.push(r)
        return r
    v.length()

branched(4)
@caption A branch's loop before the branch's own push sees the vector of the loops before it
@value 20
@end

== LIMITATIONS (NOT PROVEN TODAY)
These forms are often correct, but the compiler does not relate the index
to the length, so the call must handle or admit IndexNotFound:

  * an index computed inline in the guard and again in the access:
        if i + 1 < 0 or i + 1 >= xs.length(): return -1
        xs.at(i + 1)
    Each written `i + 1` is its own expression: bind it to a name once
    (`t = i + 1`) and guard and read the name.
  * arithmetic on a bounded loop variable: xs.at(i + 1) with i below
    xs.length() - 1, or xs.at(i - 1) with i from 1.
  * a descending loop: `loop i down from xs.length() - 1 through 0:`
    xs.at(i), including as a lockstep clause.
  * an index or length that crosses a function boundary: facts about a
    caller's arguments are not carried into the callee's analysis, unless
    the callee declares them (a parameter of type List[T]{length: N}).
  * an index reduced by another container's size, even one of equal size:
    `keys.at(h.mod(slots.capacity()))` (equal capacities of two arrays are
    not recorded), an offset of a reduced index (`h.mod(n) + 1`), or a mask
    with nothing proving the size is not 0 (the mask of an empty array is
    -1, and bit_and(h, -1) is h).
  * the List of a loop over a MutableVector, against the length of
    another loop over the same vector with something between the two
    loops that may change its length: a push, pop or clear on it (also in
    a branch, a handler or a loop body on the way), or -- for a context's
    vector -- a call that may reach the installed context (a function that
    requires it may grow the vector through its own context parameter; a
    coroutine body may too) or a yield. A copy is another vector: two
    copies taken around a push have unrelated lengths. Nor does a shorter
    length after a pop prove an index of the longer List collected before
    it: lengths are related by equality only.

@example reject
import std::list
import std::mutable_vector

fn grown(h: int) -> int:
    v = std::mutable_vector::from_list([1, 2, 3])
    ys = loop x in v:
        x * 10
    v.push(4)
    zs = loop x in v:
        x
    ys.at(h.mod(zs.length()))

0
@caption Two loops over one vector, a push between them
@error CORE SEMANTIC UNHANDLED-ERROR
@at 11:5
@end

@example reject
import std::list

fn after(xs: List[int], i: int) -> int:
    if i + 1 < 0 or i + 1 >= xs.length():
        return -1
    xs.at(i + 1)

0
@caption An index computed twice, in the guard and in the read
@error CORE SEMANTIC UNHANDLED-ERROR
@at 6:5
@end

@example reject
import std::list

fn backwards(xs: List[int]) -> List[int]:
    loop i down from xs.length() - 1 through 0:
        xs.at(i)

0
@caption A descending loop is not proven
@error CORE SEMANTIC UNHANDLED-ERROR
@at 5:9
@end

@example reject
import std::mutable_array

fn masked(a: MutableArray[int], h: int) -> int:
    a.at(bit_and(h, a.capacity() - 1))

0
@caption A mask with no guard that the capacity is not 0
@error CORE SEMANTIC UNHANDLED-ERROR
@at 4:5
@end

== WHAT TO DO WHEN THE PROOF FAILS
Restate the access in a recognized form when the code allows it: write the
container the same way in the guard and the access; bind a computed index to
a name once and guard that name; iterate ascending (or iterate the elements,
`loop x in xs:`, when the index is not needed); use lockstep (`loop x in xs
and i from 0 to xs.length():`) for an element and its index. Otherwise make
the outcome explicit: handle IndexNotFound where the read is (`xs.at(t): on
IndexNotFound: fallback`), or use std::list::get(xs, i, default), which is
that handler written once (stdlib.std::list::get). Admitting the error in
the function's `errors` clause passes the decision to the callers.

@example accept
import std::list

fn at_or(xs: List[int], t: int, fallback: int) -> int:
    xs.at(t):
        on IndexNotFound:
            fallback

fn after(xs: List[int], i: int) -> int:
    t = i + 1
    xs.at_or(t, -1)

[1, 2, 3].after(1)
@caption Handled where the read is
@value 3
@end

