== LANGUAGE GUARANTEE
A slice START..END of a sequence of N elements is valid iff 0 <= START <=
END <= N. START is checked first, against 0..N, then END against START..N: a
bound below its interval fails with LowerUnderrun, one above it with
UpperOverrun (so an inverted slice is LowerUnderrun). Both are builtin
declared errors: handle them, admit them, or be proven unable to raise them.
std::str::substring counts characters (Unicode scalar values), the index
space of std::str::length and std::str::char_at.

== RECOGNIZED PATTERNS (PROVEN TODAY)
  * a literal String (or an immutable binding of one) and constant bounds;
  * a length guard: `if s.length() > 0: s.substring(0, 1)`;
  * a guard on a parameter against the String's length:
    `if n < 0 or n > s.length(): return ""` then `s.substring(0, n)`;
  * a counted loop over the String's length: `loop i from 0 to s.length():
    s.substring(i, i + 1)`;
  * lengths of std::str::concat results (the sum of the parts' lengths), and
    a MutableArray's capacity for freeze and copy;
  * a String or MutableArray reached through a field path, written the same
    way in the guard and in the slice (`p.name.length()` ...
    `p.name.substring(n, p.name.length())`).

@example accept
import std::str

fn prefix(s: str, n: int) -> str:
    if n < 0 or n > s.length():
        return ""
    s.substring(0, n)

"abc".prefix(2)
@caption A guarded parameter as the end of the slice
@value "ab"
@end

== LIMITATIONS (NOT PROVEN TODAY)
A bound computed into a local and then guarded (unlike an index: a guard on
a computed local proves no slice), and, as for indexed access
(proof.list-bounds), a bound that depends on a value the analysis cannot
relate to the length. A slice whose start is an unconstrained parameter
cannot be proven:

@example reject
import std::str

fn tail(s: str, n: int) -> str:
    s.substring(n, s.length())

0
@caption An unconstrained start
@error CORE SEMANTIC UNHANDLED-ERROR
@at 4:5
@end

== WHAT TO DO
Guard the parameter (`if n < 0 or n > s.length(): return ""`), handle the
two errors at the call, or admit them in the function's `errors` clause.

@topic proof.completions
title: Completion proofs: which errors a call can raise
summary: For each call the compiler computes the errors it may raise from the facts proven there: a call that cannot fail needs no handler; one that always fails is rejected (KNOWN-ERROR).
index: proofs concepts
alias: completion proof; completion analysis; provable success; error analysis; static completion
keywords: completion, errors, handler, fail, proof, known error, success
related: concept.errors concept.known-errors rule.unhandled-errors rule.known-error error.unhandled-error error.known-error proof.list-bounds proof.slices proof.ranges proof.callable-contracts concept.negative-tests
docs: STATIC-COMPLETION-PROOFS.md EXPLICIT-ERROR-COMPLETIONS.md ERROR-IDENTITY-AND-COMPLETION-SOUNDNESS.md

== LANGUAGE GUARANTEE
A function completes normally (with its value), with one of the declared
errors its `errors` clause names, or not at all. Every error a call may
raise must be handled at the call (`on NAME:`) or admitted by the enclosing
function's `errors` clause; nothing propagates silently.

== WHAT THE COMPILER PROVES
For each call, the compiler evaluates the callee's completions under the
facts proven for the call's arguments there (constants, ranges, guards,
lengths), interprocedurally, with a budget:
  * an error the callee cannot raise with these arguments is no
    obligation: `risky(3)` needs no handler when `risky` fails only for
    negative arguments;
  * when the call cannot complete normally at all -- it always raises -- the
    program is rejected (CORE SEMANTIC KNOWN-ERROR), handler or not: a
    statically known failure is a bug in the program, not a run-time
    situation to handle (concept.known-errors);
  * otherwise the call must deal with the errors that remain possible.
The analysis is sound in the conservative direction: when it cannot decide,
the error stays possible.

@example accept
error Bad

fn risky(x: int) -> int errors Bad:
    if x < 0:
        fail Bad
    x

risky(3)
@caption Provable success: no handler needed
@value 3
@end

@example reject
error Bad

fn risky(x: int) -> int errors Bad:
    if x < 0:
        fail Bad
    x

risky(-3):
    on Bad:
        -1
@caption Provable failure is rejected, even with a handler
@error CORE SEMANTIC KNOWN-ERROR
@at 8:1
@end

== RECOGNIZED PATTERNS
Facts the analysis carries into a call: exact constants and immutable
bindings of them (proof.exact-values), integer ranges from comparisons and
domains (proof.ranges), list and String lengths and their guards
(proof.list-bounds, proof.slices), refinement proofs (proof.refinements),
and the contracts of function values (proof.callable-contracts).

== LIMITATIONS
  * A fact must be about a value the analysis tracks at the call: a
    parameter, a constant, a binding of one. Facts about a caller's
    arguments are not carried into a callee's own body (each function body
    is checked for every argument).
  * Arithmetic relations between values (`i + 1 < n` from `i < n - 1`) are
    not derived.
  * Whether a call fails is decided from the callee's ordering comparisons
    of an Int (`<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`, combined with and, or and not), not
    from `==`: after `fn f(n: int) -> int errors C: if n == 7: fail C`,
    both f(7) and f(3) may fail as far as the compiler knows (f(3) needs a
    handler, and f(7) is not KNOWN-ERROR). The same guard written
    `n >= 7 and n <= 7` is decided at both calls.
  * Recursion and large programs are bounded by a budget: beyond it, errors
    stay possible.

== TESTING A FAILURE
Because a literal failing call is rejected, a test of a failure passes the
failing input through a parameter, so the call is not statically known to
fail (concept.negative-tests).

@topic proof.ranges
title: Integer ranges and integer domains
summary: The compiler tracks the range of every Int from constants, comparisons and arithmetic, and uses it to prove declared integer domains (`type Digit = Int in 0..9`), bounds and decided branches.
index: proofs
alias: integer range; range analysis; integer domain proof; value range
keywords: range, Int, domain, comparison, guard, facts
related: concept.integer-domains proof.list-bounds proof.completions proof.reachability proof.exact-values
docs: SOURCE-DEFINED-INTEGER-DOMAINS.md RANGE-FIXPOINT-SCALING.md M6-RANGE-DECIDED-BRANCH-LOWERING.md

== LANGUAGE GUARANTEE
A value of an integer domain (`type Byte = Int in 0..255`) is always within
it: every place that produces one -- a function result declared as the
domain, an argument to such a parameter -- must be proven in range at
compile time. No run-time range check or wrap-around is inserted.

== RECOGNIZED PATTERNS
Ranges come from literals, + - * on ranges, comparisons in conditions
(`if n < 0 or n > 9: return 0` leaves 0..9 on the fall-through, also through
`and`, `or`, `not`), counted loop variables, domain-typed values and
registered native results.

@example accept
type Digit = Int in 0..9

fn digit(n: int) -> Digit:
    if n < 0 or n > 9:
        return 0
    n

digit(5)
@caption A guard proves the domain
@value 5
@end

@example reject
type Digit = Int in 0..9

fn digit(n: int) -> Digit:
    n

0
@caption An unconstrained Int is not a Digit
@error CORE SEMANTIC TYPE
@at 3:9
@end

== LIMITATIONS
Relations between two variables are not tracked (`i < j` proves nothing
about i alone), nor facts that cross a function boundary into the callee's
body; a range is an interval, so `n != 5` adds nothing. Deciding whether a
call fails reads the callee's ordering comparisons, not `==`
(proof.completions, LIMITATIONS).

@topic proof.types
title: Static types: proven, never checked at run time
summary: A value meets a declared type (parameter, result, field) only if the compiler proves it: "cannot be proven to satisfy" means the proof failed; no run-time check stands behind it.
index: proofs concepts
alias: type proof; cannot be proven to satisfy; does not prove declared type
keywords: type, inference, declared, annotation, any, prove, satisfy
related: concept.types error.type rule.typed-parameters proof.ranges proof.refinements
docs: README.md STRICT-TYPED-PARAMETERS.md SYMBOLIC-TYPE-IDENTITY.md

== LANGUAGE GUARANTEE
A declared parameter type, result type or struct field type holds for every
value that reaches it on every backend: the compiler proves it at every
call, return and construction. An unannotated parameter accepts any value
and its function is checked for the kinds of values its calls pass.

== WHAT THE COMPILER PROVES
Types flow from literals, declarations, operations and the results of
calls; conditions narrow them (refinements, ranges). A mismatch is reported
where the value meets the declaration ("argument for parameter "n" cannot
be proven to satisfy int (argument type: str)"; "function result does not
prove declared type str").

@example reject
fn twice(n: int) -> int:
    n * 2

twice("x")
@error CORE SEMANTIC TYPE
@at 4:7
@end

== LIMITATIONS
A value whose type is unknown statically (from an untyped generic
parameter, `any`) does not prove a specific type: annotate the source of the
value, or narrow it with a type test the compiler recognizes. Native
operations check their argument kinds at run time too.

@topic proof.refinements
title: Refinement proofs: predicates and validators
summary: A `proves` predicate refines its argument in the branch where it returned true; a validator refines it for the rest of the path once it returned; nothing else makes a refined value.
index: proofs
alias: refinement proof; predicate proof; validator proof
keywords: proves, refined, predicate, validator, narrowing, if
related: concept.refinements warning.proves-naming proof.types principle.predicate-naming
docs: REFINEMENT-VALUES.md

== LANGUAGE GUARANTEE
A value of a refinement type (`refined type Small = int`) was proven to be
one: by a predicate (`fn small?(n: int) -> bool proves n: Small`) that
returned true for it, or by a validator (`-> unit proves n: Small errors
E`) that returned normally. A refined value has its carrier's
representation; there is no run-time tag.

== RECOGNIZED PATTERNS
  * `if small?(n):` -- n is Small in the then-branch;
  * `ok = small?(n)` then `if ok:`;
  * through `not`, `and`, `or`: `if not small?(n): return 0` leaves n Small
    on the fall-through;
  * a validator call: after `validate_small(n)` returns, n is Small (a
    handled call keeps the fact only if every handler that completes proves
    it too).

@example accept
refined type Small = int

fn small?(n: int) -> bool proves n: Small:
    n < 10

fn use(n: Small) -> int:
    n

fn f(n: int) -> int:
    if not small?(n):
        return 0
    use(n)

f(3)
@value 3
@end

== LIMITATIONS
Facts are tied to the binding the predicate was called with; a copy made
before the test is not refined. Only the declaring module of a refinement
may write a `proves` clause for it.

@topic proof.lockstep
title: Lockstep loops: proving equal iteration counts
summary: A lockstep loop (`loop x in xs and i from 0 to xs.length():`) compiles only when the compiler proves every clause runs the same number of times; there is no shortest-wins rule and no run-time check.
index: proofs
alias: lockstep proof; equal iteration counts; zip proof
keywords: lockstep, and, loop, length, cardinality, zip
related: concept.lockstep error.lockstep-unproven error.lockstep-unequal concept.loops
docs: COLLECTING-LOOPS.md

== RECOGNIZED PATTERNS
The count of a list clause is its list's length; of a numeric clause its
bounds. Equal counts are proven when they are the same length form
(`xs` and `0 to xs.length()`), constants, or lists of known length.

@example accept
import std::list

fn indexed(xs: List[int]) -> List[int]:
    loop x in xs and i from 0 to xs.length():
        x + i

indexed([5, 6])
@value [5, 7]
@end

@example reject
fn pairs(xs: List[int], ys: List[int]) -> List[int]:
    loop x in xs and y in ys:
        x + y

0
@caption Two unrelated lists
@error CORE SEMANTIC LOCKSTEP-UNPROVEN
@at 2:5
@end

== LIMITATIONS
Two different lists are unrelated even when the caller passes lists of the
same length. So are two loops over one MutableVector with something
between them that may change its length (a push, pop or clear on it; for
a context's vector, also a call that may reach the installed context, or a
yield), or
such a vector and the List an earlier loop over it collected: the
vector's length changes in place, so each walk counts the vector as it is
then. With nothing of the kind between them -- the pushes all before both,
or after both, or in the lockstep loop's own body, which runs after its
clauses are evaluated -- the vector and the List have one length, as do
two clauses naming the same vector (`loop a in v and b in v`).

@example reject
import std::mutable_vector

fn widened() -> List[int]:
    v = std::mutable_vector::from_list([1, 2, 3])
    ys = loop x in v:
        x * 10
    v.push(4)
    loop a in v and y in ys:
        a + y

0
@caption A vector grown after the List was collected from it
@error CORE SEMANTIC LOCKSTEP-UNPROVEN
@at 8:5
@end

@topic proof.contexts
title: Context requirements
summary: The compiler derives every function's transitive context requirements from the call graph and proves that each top-level call runs after the installation of every context it needs.
index: proofs
alias: context proof; context requirement; missing context proof
keywords: context, with context, requirement, install, MISSING-CONTEXT
related: concept.contexts concept.context-traits error.missing-context rule.context-installation
docs: CONTEXTS.md CONTEXT-TRAITS.md

== LANGUAGE GUARANTEE
A function that uses a context declares it (`context io: LinuxIO`); its
callers need it too, without saying so; a call at the top level must come
after `with context EXPR` for every context it transitively needs, and a
context type is installed at most once.

== WHAT IS REPORTED
MISSING-CONTEXT names the call and the chain of calls that explains the
requirement; -contexts prints every function's requirements and why.

@example reject
import std::io
import std::linux::io

fn hello() errors std::io::WriteFailed:
    std::io::print_line("hi")

hello():
    on std::io::WriteFailed:
        unit
with context std::linux::io::create()
0
@caption The call comes before the installation
@error CORE SEMANTIC MISSING-CONTEXT
@at 7:1
@end

@topic proof.ownership
title: Ownership of affine values
summary: An affine value (a coroutine handle, or an aggregate holding one) has one usable owner: the compiler proves every use comes before the value moves, and releases it where its owner dies.
index: proofs
alias: ownership proof; affine proof; use after move proof
keywords: affine, move, release, coroutine, ownership, drop
related: concept.affine concept.coroutines rule.affine-use error.use-after-move
docs: AFFINE-VALUES.md COROUTINES.md

== LANGUAGE GUARANTEE
An affine value is never copied: binding it to another name, passing it,
storing it in a struct moves it, and a use of the old name afterwards is
rejected (USE-AFTER-MOVE). It is released after its last use or when its
scope is left early; no run-time ownership state exists.

== LIMITATIONS
Several places an affine value could live are not supported yet (captures,
some containers, generic nesting): the AFFINE-*-UNSUPPORTED diagnostics
name them (-help errors).

@topic proof.callable-contracts
title: Calls through function values
summary: A call of a function value is bounded by its structural type Fn{args, return, errors}, checked where the value acquires it; an untyped function value is charged every error it may carry.
index: proofs
alias: callable contract; function value errors; Fn contract proof
keywords: Fn, callable, function value, contract, errors, higher-order
related: concept.function-types concept.closures proof.completions error.unhandled-error
docs: VERIFIED-CALLABLE-CONTRACT-BOUNDARY.md STRUCTURAL-FUNCTION-TYPES.md STATIC-COMPLETION-PROOFS.md

== LANGUAGE GUARANTEE
A parameter typed `Fn{args: [int], return: int, errors: [E]}` admits only
functions that take an int, return an int and raise at most E; every call
through it may raise exactly the declared errors. A function value without
a structural type is charged conservatively.

== IN PRACTICE
Declare the Fn type of callback parameters: callers then handle exactly
the declared errors, and the callback's own errors are checked against the
contract where it is passed.

@topic proof.reachability
title: Reachability and decided branches
summary: Code after a completion that cannot finish, and branches whose condition the compiler decides, are unreachable; the analyses and warnings ignore what can never run.
index: proofs
alias: dead code; unreachable code; decided branch
keywords: reachable, unreachable, dead code, decided, condition
related: proof.ranges proof.exact-values warning.same-return-value
docs: M6-RANGE-DECIDED-BRANCH-LOWERING.md

== WHAT THE COMPILER PROVES
A statement after `return`, `fail`, `break` or `continue`, or after a call
that always fails, is unreachable; a condition the compiler can decide from
constants and ranges (`if 1 == 2:`) makes the other branch unreachable.
Warnings do not count unreachable exits; the native backends compile only
the reachable branch.

== LIMITATIONS
Unreachability that depends on facts across functions, or on facts that
contradict each other, is not always recognized: the code is then still
checked.

@topic proof.exact-values
title: Exact values and constants
summary: The compiler knows the exact value of literals, immutable bindings of them and simple operations on known values, and uses it in completion proofs, bounds proofs and warnings.
index: proofs
alias: exact value; constant folding; known value
keywords: constant, literal, exact, binding, immutable
related: proof.completions proof.ranges proof.list-bounds warning.same-return-value
docs: EXACT-VALUE-FACTS.md

== WHAT THE COMPILER PROVES
Literals (Int, Bool, String, character, small Lists of them), immutable
bindings of known values (aliases included: `a = 2`, `b = a`), and
+ - * of known Ints are exact. A literal list's length and elements are
known, so `xs = [1, 2, 3]` then `xs.at(2)` needs no handler.

== LIMITATIONS
Struct values have no exact facts; Lists past a small size or depth, calls,
and anything computed at run time are not exact.
